The OLED comprises:

- a substrate;
- a first electrode;
- an organic electroluminescent layer stack having a defined lateral contour;
- a second electrode with relatively low lateral conductivity; and
- a highly conductive current-distribution layer electrically connected to the second electrode only at selected contact regions located outside the contour of the organic layer stack.

Because current enters the lower-conductivity electrode through these peripheral, selectively positioned contacts, the electrode develops a controlled lateral voltage drop. The resulting current density through the organic stack—and therefore the emitted luminance—varies predictably across the OLED area. By choosing the number, position, spacing, and shape of the external contact regions, the device can generate a desired light distribution, such as uniform illumination, a gradient, or predefined bright and dim zones, without patterning the emissive organic layers themselves.
